hello everyone welcome to Paul and my  name is the Lang focused channel several  months ago I released a video on the  French language and more recently I  released a video on cockney rhyming  slang and that got me thinking about  french slang yes despite all the efforts  of the French Academy l'académie  française to promote proper French or  formal french french slang does exist  and is commonly used one interesting  type of French slang is called villain  that named villain comes from the word  long their meaning backwards or inverse  the word syllables are reversed and we  get that long the name of this type of  slang contains an example of the slang  Vermont is a kind of metathesis side by  side sounds switching positions and in  this type the basic idea is that two  syllables in a word switch places with  each other  note that this kind of slang originates  in the spoken language so the spelling  of the slang word comes from its sound  rather than vice-versa the S at the end  of long veil is silent that's why it's  not part of the word that long I think  you all know the standard French word  Mel C in Vietnam it's C mayor I'm sure  you also know the word Bonjour  can you guess what it would be in  Vietnam yes it's jus of bone then  there's the word see to you in Vietnam  it's two three  then there's the word Metro the metro or  subway in Vietnam it's ptomaine and the  word bazaar meaning weird or bizarre in  Vietnam it's zombie so where did this  slang come from and who uses it that law  began to arise in the decades following  World War two in the poor suburbs of  Paris  since then it has entered mainstream  culture through music TV movies and so  on it's still used most heavily by young  people in Livonia low-income suburbs of  France's major cities where mostly  immigrants and their descendants live  but a certain amount of Vietnam is used  by the wider mainstream population  especially young people this sentence  contains one of the most mainstream  violent words mo MEC Mott Lizabeth  Donohue this means my boyfriend looks at  other girls on the street the  long word here is must this is an  inversion of the word family so it  generally means woman or girl but in the  phrase mammoth it means my girlfriend as  opposed to ma femme  which means my wife I guess the  formality of the relationship is  reflected in the formality of the word  the word Mick is not very long but is  also slang which means something like  guy or dude and mum MEC means my  boyfriend in Paris though you might hear  people say kun which is a very long  version of MEK but that one is less  common than myth it might not be  immediately obvious how these single  syllable words become reversed at the  end of the word femme for instance there  is a release after the M consonant like  a schwa FEM that ma at the end becomes  the first syllable in the reversed word  and fact becomes the second syllable but  then the final vowel is dropped the  schwa sound from gets spelled as EU and  the vowel sound naturally changes a  little bit this is how most single  syllable verloc words are formed a few  more examples  Mayer meaning mother becomes boom jab  beat shimma whom this sentence means I  live at my mom's place pear meaning  father becomes look fat meaning party  becomes too unfailing to fellows on me  are we having a party at home in this  sentence contains one more very long  word soul may which is a reversal of  mais own the word meaning house  sometimes not just a vowel but a  consonant might disappear if it's  awkward to pronounce there's the word  fleek  which means cop like a police officer  and the Vermont word is cuff you might  be expecting it to sound like could flee  but the final syllable gets dropped and  it's just cooked at all Co  voila couth this means watch out the  cops are here  vlad by the way is an abbreviated form  of voila another common change that  happens is that a sound is removed  before the inversion takes place so we  have the word legal a meaning to laughs  which becomes goalie  so the ER ending was removed from the  word before reversing the two syllables  the innovators of slang normally stay  one step ahead of the general population  and when a word or expression becomes  two common they often stop using it in  the case of a law some words have become  Reaver on eyes door like double their  long before I mentioned the word fleek  which became verlan eyes to cook but  that word has become very well-known so  people verlan eyes dit again to [ __ ]  maybe it was Reaver lan eyes because the  police started to understand the word  cook and people wanted to talk about the  police without them understanding  another example the Vermont version of  the word a hub meaning Arab is BER and  this is one of the most well known  Vermont words  it became Reaver LAN eyes Daz Harbor and  move from family woman sometimes gets  Reaver LAN eyes to the moon use of that  law is not constant or consistent it's  usually limited to a couple of words in  each sentence and those same words are  not always used in their slang form also  when verbs are villainized  they can't be conjugated so verbs are  only used in these forms the infinitive  the present tense the imperative and  also the past participle so for example  for the word goalie from legal a -  laughs you can say jiggly meaning I  laugh  jiggly meaning I laughed with this  functioning as the past participle or  Java goalie I'm going to laugh with this  being the infinitive form you could also  use the imperative form goalie but the  meaning of that imperative form would be  kind of strange for this work for any of  the other conjugations you need to use  the standard verb legally and conjugate  it for example for the conditional  present form you would use illegal fa  sol ver claw is somewhat limited in when  it can be used now let's check out a few  examples of very long in context these  examples all come from the French film  called lion this movie